Auto-generated legacy ServiceAccount token Secrets (those bi-directionally referenced by a ServiceAccount via its `secrets` field) are cleaned up by the legacy ServiceAccount token cleaner. It runs in kube-controller-manager and checks every 24 hours.

It first reads the tracking ConfigMap created by the control plane, which requires LegacyServiceAccountTokenTracking. If enough time has passed since the date in that ConfigMap, it evaluates every Secret of type `kubernetes.io/service-account-token`.

A Secret is marked invalid if all of these hold:
- it is auto-generated;
- it is not mounted by any pod;
- it has not been used for the configured period since it was created or last used.

Marking means adding the label `kubernetes.io/legacy-token-invalid-since` with the current date. If the invalidated Secret then stays unused for the same period again, it is deleted.

The period defaults to one year and is set with the kube-controller-manager flag `--legacy-service-account-token-clean-up-period`. Per the docs, this behaviour applies beginning from 1.29.

If someone uses an invalidated token, the validator:
- adds the audit annotation `authentication.k8s.io/legacy-token-invalidated`;
- increments the `invalid_legacy_auto_token_uses_total` metric;
- updates the `kubernetes.io/legacy-token-last-used` label;
- returns an error.

Users can remove the invalid-since label to temporarily allow the token again. The KEP adds that if the tracking ConfigMap is unavailable, no Secret is removed.

Kubernetes did not actually drop the --oidc-* flags. They continue to work and are still supported, and the file-based configuration is backwards-compatible with them. However, you cannot combine the two: specifying --authentication-config together with any --oidc-* flag is a misconfiguration, and the API server reports an error and exits. SIG Auth recommends migrating to the file.

The stated reason for adding the file is that the flag-based OIDC authenticator could not provide customizations users wanted. KEP-3331's goals list:
- claim validation rules (the flags only allowed audience validation, by exact value);
- richer claim mappings, including nested claims (the flags could only pick a single value from a single claim and prefix groups);
- more than one OIDC provider and more than one client ID, instead of needing an external multiplexer such as Dex;
- changing authenticator settings without restarting kube-apiserver;
- validation rules on the final user info object, so infrastructure providers can safely expose this to customers;
- easy migration from the existing OIDC flags.

The Kubernetes blog adds that a configuration file gives more flexibility than command-line options and makes it easier to deliver further improvements in later releases. The v1.34 release post says the AuthenticationConfiguration format moves away from a large set of command-line options. It also says it improves the manageability and auditability of authentication settings, with multiple JWT authenticators, CEL expression validation and dynamic reloading.
